{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"## Answer

Nitrogen became the dominant atmospheric gas through a combination of planetary chemistry, atmospheric escape, volcanism, and the comparatively weak reactivity of molecular nitrogen.

### 1. Why nitrogen accumulated

Earth’s first atmosphere was not simply the atmosphere observed today. Light gases such as hydrogen and helium were readily lost to space, while Earth’s gravity retained heavier volatile compounds. Subsequent volcanic and hydrothermal outgassing supplied nitrogen, water vapour, carbon dioxide, methane and sulfur-bearing gases.

Nitrogen was especially likely to accumulate because atmospheric \(N_2\) is chemically stable. Its two atoms are joined by a very strong triple bond, so ordinary atmospheric chemistry does not readily convert it into other compounds. By contrast:

- hydrogen escaped efficiently;
- carbon dioxide was progressively dissolved, weathered into carbonate minerals, buried as organic carbon and carbonate, and recycled through tectonics;
- sulfur gases were oxidized and deposited;
- oxygen, once produced by photosynthesis, reacted with reduced minerals, volcanic gases and organic matter before becoming abundant.

Thus, nitrogen was not necessarily added faster than every other gas; much of the competing atmospheric inventory was removed faster.

The present atmosphere contains about 78% \(N_2\) by volume and approximately \(3.9\times10^{18}\) kg of atmospheric nitrogen. Its enormous reservoir and chemical inertness give it a calculated residence time on the order of \(10^7\) years, far longer than the residence times of reactive nitrogen compounds. [Hobbs, as reproduced by Iowa State University, *Equilibrium and actual concentrations of constituents of the Earth’s atmosphere*](https://meteor.geol.iastate.edu/gccourse/chem/evol/images/image9.html); [Hudak et al., 2024](https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1029/2024GL111312).

### 2. Geological mechanisms

The long-term nitrogen inventory is controlled by exchange among atmosphere, biosphere, oceans, crust and mantle.

Volcanic and hydrothermal degassing transferred nitrogen from the interior to the surface. Nitrogen was also released when nitrogen-bearing minerals and organic matter were metamorphosed or weathered. Conversely, biological fixation converted \(N_2\) into ammonium and other usable compounds. Those compounds could be buried in sediments and carried into the mantle by subduction.

This creates a geological cycle:

\[
N_2(\text{atmosphere})
\rightarrow
NH_4^+,\ NO_3^-(\text{fixed nitrogen})
\rightarrow
\text{biomass and sediments}
\rightarrow
\text{subduction}
\rightarrow
\text{metamorphic/volcanic release}
\rightarrow
N_2
\]

Recent measurements estimate nitrogen outgassing from volcanic arcs at approximately \(4.0\times10^{10}\) mol per year and indicate that roughly half of the nitrogen subducted at modern arcs may remain in the mantle. This suggests that modern Earth may have net geological nitrogen ingassing, although the magnitude and history of the deep nitrogen cycle remain uncertain. [Hudak et al., 2024](https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1029/2024GL111312).

The biological cycle is equally important. Nitrogen-fixing microbes use nitrogenase to reduce \(N_2\) to ammonia. Nitrification converts ammonium into nitrate, while denitrification and anammox return fixed nitrogen to atmospheric \(N_2\). The key consequence is that biology moves nitrogen between reservoirs but normally does not permanently destroy it. Permanent atmospheric depletion occurs mainly when fixed nitrogen is buried in sediments or transferred into the crust and mantle.

This explains why nitrogen can dominate the atmosphere even though biology continually removes some \(N_2\): fixation is largely offset over long periods by denitrification and geological return, while the atmospheric reservoir is vastly larger than the annual fluxes. Marine nitrogen cycling has existed for billions of years, with biological fixation, nitrification, denitrification and anammox coupled to changing ocean redox conditions. [Penn et al., 2024, *Nature Reviews Earth & Environment*](https://www.nature.com/articles/s43017-024-00591-5).

### 3. Effect of industrial fixation

The Haber–Bosch process converts atmospheric \(N_2\) into ammonia:

\[
N_2 + 3H_2 \rightarrow 2NH_3
\]

Industrial fixation therefore does not create new nitrogen; it transfers nitrogen from the atmospheric reservoir into reactive chemical forms. Some is incorporated into crops and animals, some is returned to the atmosphere by denitrification, and some is lost to rivers, groundwater, coastal waters, soils and sediments. A substantial fraction of anthropogenic reactive nitrogen escapes the intended agricultural system. [Battye et al., 2017](https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/2017EF000592).

Global agricultural nitrogen-fertilizer use reached approximately 112 million tonnes of nitrogen in 2023, while total inorganic-fertilizer production was about 120 million tonnes of nitrogen. FAO reports that nitrogen-fertilizer use increased 32% between 2002 and 2023. [Food and Agriculture Organization, 2025](https://www.fao.org/statistics/highlights-archive/highlights-detail/inorganic-fertilizers-2002-2023/).

A commonly used FAO-associated baseline has projected roughly a 50% increase in fertilizer demand between 2012 and 2050, although actual demand will depend on population, diets, crop yields, fertilizer efficiency, policy and decarbonization of ammonia production. [Sutton et al., 2021](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9411506/).

### 4. Likely atmospheric concentration in 2050

The important distinction is between:

1. industrial fixation rate;
2. atmospheric \(N_2\) removal;
3. net permanent sequestration.

These are not equal. If fertilizer use rises, most of the additional nitrogen will enter the reactive nitrogen cycle rather than remain permanently outside the atmosphere. Denitrification returns a large fraction to \(N_2\), and the atmosphere contains roughly \(3.9\times10^6\) million tonnes of nitrogen.

For scale, even assuming an additional average industrial fixation of 50–60 million tonnes of nitrogen per year from now to 2050, the gross transfer would be approximately:

\[
(50\text{–}60)\times 24
\approx 1{,}200\text{–}1{,}440\ \text{Mt N}
\]

Relative to the atmospheric inventory:

\[
\frac{1{,}200\text{–}1{,}440}{3{,}900{,}000}
\approx 0.03\text{–}0.04\%
\]

That is an intentionally extreme upper-bound transfer because it treats the extra fixation as if it produced no return flux. In reality, denitrification, combustion, ocean exchange and geological cycling would offset much of it.

Therefore, under a continuation of the current industrial trajectory, the most defensible projection for 2050 is:

- atmospheric nitrogen remains approximately **78% of dry air**;
- its absolute \(N_2\) inventory may decline slightly if burial and long-term retention exceed return fluxes;
- the change in concentration is likely **well below one percentage point**, plausibly only a few hundredths of a percentage point;
- the much larger effect will be an increase in reactive nitrogen in soils, waters and ecosystems, together with increased \(N_2O\), ammonia, nitrate pollution and eutrophication.

A reasonable central estimate is therefore approximately **78.0–78.1% \(N_2\) in 2050**, not a dramatic atmospheric transformation. The uncertainty in this estimate is substantial because global fixation, denitrification, sediment burial and mantle exchange are each imperfectly constrained. Industrial fixation is likely to strongly perturb the *reactive* nitrogen cycle while barely changing the percentage of atmospheric \(N_2\) over only 24 years.

**Conclusion:** Nitrogen became dominant because it was retained by Earth, supplied by outgassing, and chemically inert relative to gases that were lost or sequestered. Continued industrial fixation will probably reduce the atmospheric \(N_2\) reservoir only marginally by 2050; its principal consequence will be redistribution and accumulation of reactive nitrogen, not a major change in atmospheric composition."}}
