C3 and C4 plants reduce photorespiration by different mechanisms:

- **C3 plants** fix atmospheric CO₂ directly with Rubisco in mesophyll chloroplasts. In heat, stomatal closure and the temperature-driven decline in Rubisco’s CO₂/O₂ specificity increase oxygenation of RuBP, so photorespiration rises. C3 plants can partly compensate through higher Rubisco specificity, photorespiratory CO₂ refixation, leaf cooling through transpiration, and acclimation of Rubisco activase and electron transport. These mechanisms reduce—but do not eliminate—photorespiration. ([Sage, 2013](https://doi.org/10.1111/plb.12024); ([Sage & Kubien, 2007](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1365-3040.2007.01682.x))

- **C4 plants** spatially separate carbon fixation. PEP carboxylase first captures CO₂ in mesophyll cells as a four-carbon acid; that acid is transported to bundle-sheath cells, where CO₂ is released around Rubisco. The resulting high bundle-sheath CO₂ concentration strongly suppresses Rubisco oxygenation and photorespiration. C4 plants therefore generally have higher water-use efficiency and a higher photosynthetic thermal optimum—often around 38–45°C—though they pay an extra ATP cost for the CO₂-concentrating cycle. ([Sage, 2013](https://doi.org/10.1111/plb.12024); [Sage & Kubien, 2007](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1365-3040.2007.01682.x))

### Projection for sub-Saharan Africa by 2045

**Under the question’s “extreme heat” framing, C4 vegetation is the more likely pathway to show higher biomass productivity in hot, seasonally dry parts of sub-Saharan Africa by 2045.** Its CO₂-concentrating mechanism gives it a direct advantage when high temperature, high light, low intercellular CO₂, and drought make photorespiration costly.

However, this is a **conditional projection, not a continent-wide certainty**. “Sub-Saharan Africa” contains humid forests, tropical savannas, drylands, irrigated cropland, and highland regions. Productivity will be controlled not only by temperature but also by rainfall timing, soil moisture, nutrients, fire, land use, and atmospheric CO₂. Observational and model studies find that precipitation and soil-moisture stress are major controls on African rangeland productivity, while warming and rainfall variability can reduce it. ([Cai et al., 2024](https://doi.org/10.1038/s43247-024-01664-5))

